* T-9: the metasystem transition, in its simplest observable form.
*
* `Run` below is an interpreter for a tiny metacoded language of list
* transducers. `Go` applies it to ONE KNOWN object program and ONE UNKNOWN
* input. Driving watches the interpreter's executions and emits a residual
* program in which the interpreter is gone: what remains is the object
* program itself, translated into Refal.
*
* That translation is the metasystem transition. The interpreter is a system
* acting on a program; the supercompiler is a system one level up, acting on
* the interpreter's runs; the residual is the new level (Turchin 1980 5.5;
* 1986 "The Concept of a Supercompiler"; 1996 "Metacomputation: MST plus
* Supercompilation").
*
* Object language, metacoded as Refal terms (Turchin 1980 1.3):
*   (End)             contribute nothing more
*   (In)              contribute the rest of the input
*   (Lit c (P))       contribute the symbol c, then run P
*   (Seq (P) (Q))     run P, then run Q
*
* The object program here is  Seq(Lit 'h' (Lit 'i' (End)), In)
* and the residual this file drives to is   e.Input = 'h' 'i' e.Input;

$ENTRY Go {
  e.In = <Run (Seq (Lit 'h' (Lit 'i' (End))) (In)) e.In>;
}

Run {
  (End) e.In = ;
  (In) e.In = e.In;
  (Lit s.C t.P) e.In = s.C <Run t.P e.In>;
  (Seq t.P t.Q) e.In = <Run t.P e.In> <Run t.Q e.In>;
}
